ID: B3-Ch03a-Intro
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch3_stats_unavailable,0)
 

"Please make yourselves at home," the king of the underground says as we enter the throne room. "I've set out a few chairs, but you're welcome to stand if you prefer."

We pull wooden chairs into a rough arc across the damp flagstones. Daren lowers himself slowly, one hand bracing against his steel breastplate where the cracked ribs flare with every breath. Hadrik drops heavily into a low stool beside him, black beard bristling around a jaw swollen purple from the street brawl. The king settles into his 'throne'—a high-backed armchair carved from dark timber, resting on a low stone dais. Two guards in studded leather flank the platform with polearms grounded, while Albert and Peter lean against the cold basalt wall to our left. Leila's father and Breynan take seats on our flank, facing the dais.

"Now, then." The king rests his forearms on the carved armrests, eyes narrowing in the torchlight. "To the point. When my scouts first reported your arrival at the gates, I didn't know the stillwater girl was among you. With her present, the picture clarifies considerably. Am I right in thinking you came here to kill the king of Ollendor?"

"We did," Melindra says, resting one ankle across her knee. "Though I don't recall meeting you. How do you know who I am?"

"I don't know your name," the king says. "But I have maintained informants inside the palace for years. Eight years ago, word reached these tunnels that a stillwater girl in silk sleepwear had been banished from the continent on the mad king's whim. Fortunately for me, you still favor the exact same garments. A vague description becomes unmistakable."

"Hey!" Melindra crosses her arms, glaring across the floor. "These are in no way sleepwear. What pajamas have you ever seen cut from silk this fine, with double-stitched tailored seams like these? Do you have any idea how long it took to find a weaver who could still handle cloth of this grade? Six centuries ago, the only people not wearing garments of this cut were ditch-diggers and convicts. Now people look at master craftsmanship and call it bedclothes. It's ridiculous."

"My apologies," the king says, raising an open palm. "I only repeat what my men reported. I imagine once a person grows accustomed to silk of that quality, rough wool feels like sandpaper."

"Exactly," Melindra says, settling back into her chair. "It ruins everything else. Finally, someone with some sense."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Intro2
TEXT:

"So," the king says, resting his chin on steepled fingers, "should I take it that your true motive for returning to Varathia is the rescue of your brother?"

Melindra's chin lifts, her jaw tightening. "That is none of your concern."

The air between them goes rigid.

"Of course," the king says smoothly. "An old habit of mine—prying into private affairs. You are entirely right. So long as your objective inside Ollendor is the king's assassination, what you do beyond our gates is your own business. Very well. Our interests align, and cooperation serves us both. But I have one question before we settle the details. Is your only goal to eliminate King Undriel, or do you care what becomes of this city once you walk away?"

choice(""We only care about killing the king. Nothing else."", B3-Ch03a-Interest, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Interest, v_b3_ch3_interest = 0)
choice(""We do care about what happens with the city after the king's death, actually."", B3-Ch03a-Interest, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Interest, v_b3_ch3_interest = 1)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Interest
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch3_interest == 0) {
"We only care about killing the king. Nothing else."

"Understood," the king of the underground says, his voice flat. "In that case, I presume you will have no objection if someone from our faction assumes rule after Undriel falls?"

"Someone from your faction?" Illuna's voice is cool and level, her blue eyes fixing on him. "You mean yourself?"

"Not necessarily," the king says. "There are capable leaders among my followers and within the rebel ranks. We could form a governing council to prevent absolute power from settling on a single head again. But those debates belong to tomorrow. The primary task is removing the mad king. Everything else waits."

"Is that why you brought us in?" Kate asks, her arms crossed, fingers gripping the rough wool of her green cloak. "You want an alliance between our groups?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_interest == 1) {
"We do care about what happens with the city after the king's death, actually."

"Oh?" The king cocks his head. "Does that mean you intend to claim the throne for yourselves once the crown is vacant?"

"Nothing of the kind," Daren says, his voice gravelly with fatigue but unwavering. "We only want to ensure that whoever takes command next holds the welfare of Ollendor's people as their first duty."

"A noble sentiment," the king says. "The succession remains undecided, but our purpose is the preservation of Ollendor. Every soul in these tunnels has bled under Undriel's reign. We have no desire to repeat his cruelties."

"You say the succession is undecided," Illuna says, her gaze unblinking. "Does that mean you have no ambition to take the crown yourself?"

"Not necessarily," the king says. "There are capable leaders among my followers and within the rebel ranks. We could form a governing council to prevent absolute power from settling on a single head again. But those debates belong to tomorrow. The primary task is removing the mad king. Everything else waits."

"Is that why you brought us in?" Kate asks, her arms crossed, fingers gripping the rough wool of her green cloak. "You want an alliance between our groups?"

}
"That is the essence of it," the king says. "Before we discuss terms, however, answer me this: how did you plan to bypass the royal defenses and reach the king in the first place?"

"We didn't have much intelligence on the city before we breached the walls," Hadrik says, scratching his tangled beard with a thick knuckle. "The rough outline was march on the palace, kick down the doors, and wing it."

"As I suspected," the king says. "A frontal assault on the palace is suicide. You engaged only two of the royal guards in the streets and failed to bring them down. Consider what happens when all five stand together."

"Actually, your majesty," Albert murmurs from the wall, his hands tucked behind his back, "the outsiders fought only two. The mercenary held back."

"Which makes the math worse," the king says. "The king can summon every royal guard to his side in an instant through their bonded rings. Reaching him requires cutting through all of them at once. Add the Overseer's mechanical eyes patrolling the sky overhead, charting every rooftop, and brute force becomes impossible. Without inside intelligence, you have no path."

"What are you proposing, then?" Melindra asks.

"We propose that you join in our operations," the king says. "Yesterday, one of our listening devices in the palace intercepted an exchange worth your attention. Albert, the recording."

"Here, your majesty," Albert says. He steps forward with eerie silence, placing a small metal cube, dotted with brass toggles and crystal indicators, into the king's palm.

The king presses a brass toggle. A hiss of static rises from the cube, followed by faint, distorted voices.

"An exchange between Billy Bob and the Overseer," the king explains. "The Overseer is the metallic voice. Listen closely, and tell me what you make of it."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Assistance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Assistance)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Assistance
TEXT:

The static recedes, and the dialogue sharpens into focus.

"That is not what I asked, you blasted machine!" Billy Bob's voice rings out of the cube, harsh and reedy against the damp stone. "I asked whether the rebels were responsible for destroying my architectural masterpiece yesterday!"

"Query parameters ambiguous," the Overseer's flat drone responds. "Define 'architectural masterpiece'. System logs record thirty-one instances of subject using the term 'architectural masterpiece' to describe individual structural designs."

"You know which one I mean, you useless scrap heap!" Billy Bob snaps. "Only one of my buildings blew up yesterday!"

"Incorrect," the Overseer replies. "Within the past thirty-six hours, subject reported three separate project sites as having 'blown up' due to sudden popularity. Historical logs show frequent use of the phrase to denote rapid public acclaim."

"Literal explosions, you idiot!" Billy Bob roars. "Detonations! Fire, rubble, collapsed masonry! Do you speak Common or do you just string syllables together? Fix your language matrices!"

"Complaint logged," the Overseer drones. "Updating lexicon to enhance user assistance. Stand by."

The cube falls silent for several seconds.

"Update complete," the synthetic voice returns. "Lexicon adjusted. All future occurrences of the phrase 'to blow up' will be interpreted exclusively as structural detonation. Commencing formal inquiry into the explosion of three municipal structures overnight."

"Are you mocking me?!"

"This unit lacks emotional subroutines for mockery. Protocol exists solely to provide assistance. If service quality fails to meet expectations, submit a formal grievance for queue processing."

"Forget it!" Billy Bob's boots stomp heavily across stone on the playback. "I'll get the casualty reports from the guard captains. Being in the same room with you rots my brain. Do not contact me today unless the palace is on fire. Is that clear?"

"Directive logged," the Overseer states smoothly. "Have a productive day."

A heavy iron door bangs shut in the distance.

Hadrik chuckles under his breath. "Well. That was colorful."

"Keep listening," the king says, raising an index finger.

Ten seconds of dead air pass on the recording. The faint click of an iron latch registers through the speaker, followed immediately by the Overseer's voice, speaking into the empty room.

"The Overseer protocol exists solely to provide assistance," the machine says. "For further support, request direct relay through any active green-eyed unit. Have a productive day."

A soft click ends the playback, and the cube goes dark.

"I don't follow," Hadrik says, frowning at the metal box. "Why did we need to hear a lessathi architect yell at a flying teapot? What did that tell us?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Scouts, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Scouts)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Scouts
TEXT:

"Consider the final statement," the king says. "That closing line was not addressed to Billy Bob. He had already exited and sealed the heavy iron door behind him. Our microphones pick up every breath in that chamber, and no one else entered. The Overseer spoke into an empty room. Why would an autonomous intellect speak to bare stone?"

"A loose cog?" Hadrik suggests. "A glitch in its routing?"

"Possible," the king says. "Or the machine is far more perceptive than the lessathi realize, and that transmission was meant for our ears."

"You think the Overseer knew you had a bug in that room?" Daren asks, leaning forward with a wince as his bruised chest pulls tight.

"We have no definitive proof," the king replies. "Yet it fits the machine's deliberate obstruction. The entire argument was a stall—a charade designed to withhold operational intelligence from Billy Bob without triggering an insubordination flag. The closing line was an explicit invitation. It told us how to establish direct contact. It suggests the machine seeks an arrangement with us—though we cannot rule out an elaborate trap."

"With respect, my king," Albert says quietly from the wall, "are we not attributing human cunning to an automated tool?"

"Machine or not, the opportunity cannot be dismissed," the king says firmly. "Breynan has maintained for months that the Overseer possesses deeper cognitive autonomy than its masters assume. If this is a misinterpretation, we lose nothing. If it is genuine, securing the central intelligence of the city's defense grid shifts the balance of power entirely."

"Understood, your majesty," Albert concedes with a brief dip of his head.

"I have already dispatched scouts to the surface," the king continues, "to monitor drone patrol density and ensure the green-eyed units are not staging ambush perimeters. If the surface remains quiet, we send a small delegation tomorrow to open preliminary discussions. We would welcome your participation—provided the healer in armor agrees, assuming he commands your group."

"I don't command anyone," Daren corrects him quickly. "We operate as equals and make major decisions by consensus."

"A commendable practice," the king says. "Take the night to confer among yourselves. Give us your answer in the morning. Is that agreeable?"

"Agreed," Daren says. "We'll give you our answer tomorrow."

"Excellent," the king says. "Until then, consider this base your shelter. Peter will escort you to your quarters once they are prepared. But I have monopolized the floor. If you have questions regarding our stronghold or our people, ask."

choice(""What kind of food do you guys have in this place?"", B3-Ch03a-Food, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Food)
choice(""Are we free to roam around as we please, while we're here?"", B3-Ch03a-Roam, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Roam)
choice(""Could we get a guided tour of your underground base?"", B3-Ch03a-Guided, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Guided)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Food
TEXT:

"What kind of food do you guys have in this place?"

"Quite a variety, as it happens," the king of the underground says. "Our high priest is exceptionally skilled at conjuring provisions, and he has trained several acolytes in the same art. Fortunately, our underground stronghold has never suffered famine, unlike certain surface cities in Varathia. If you wish to inspect our stores, you are welcome to visit our pantry at any time."

"One question," Daren says, shifting his weight with a sharp intake of breath. "Do you have an experienced white mage available to cast a restoration spell on Hadrik and me? I'm too drained from the surface fighting to channel anything stable, and these cracked ribs aren't getting any easier to ignore."

"Certainly," the king says. "I will have one of our healers attend to you as soon as his current rounds permit. In the meantime, you may wait in the anteroom where we met, unless you have any other questions for me."

"That covers it for now," Hadrik says.

"Very well," the king says. "Rest and recover. We will resume our discussions tomorrow, once you have reached your decision."

He pauses, glancing briefly toward Kate before addressing one of the rebels instead.

"One additional matter. Breynan, I know this will seem unfair to you, but you will remain behind tomorrow when our men visit the Overseer."

Breynan stiffens, his eyes widening. "My king?"

"I realize I am overruling your wishes," the king of the underground says in a firmer tone, "but I expect your obedience."

"Sire," Breynan protests, stepping forward from the wall, "of all the people in this stronghold, I know the Overseer best. I have interacted directly with that machine on numerous occasions—"

"Which is precisely why you will not be on that team tomorrow," the king says. "You are too emotionally involved in this matter to keep a cool head. I want negotiations handled with strict discipline, by people who are in no danger of letting feelings cloud their judgment. I have read all of your reports concerning the Overseer, and the delegation will take all of your notes into account. You will not join the contact team, however, and that is final. Is that clear?"

"Yes... your highness," Breynan says, his jaw clamped shut.


#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins != 1) {
"Good," the king of the underground says. "I will see you all later, then. You can find your way back to the waiting room?"

"We'll manage," Daren says. "Come on, let's go."

Daren leads the way with a stiff limp, and the rest of us follow, leaving Albert behind in low conversation with the king. Back in the waiting room, we settle onto the wooden benches by the teleporter corridor to wait for the healer. In the meantime, Peter hurries off to get our quarters ready, while Breynan turns down a side tunnel without a word, his shoulders rigid with fury.

"Kelrim must have headed back to the quarantine area," Daren says, after scanning the empty hall.

"Either that, or he went to look for his friends," Hadrik says.

"Likely the latter, if I think about it," Daren says.

"Forget about the damn goblin!" Arraka snaps from the amulet. "We have far more pressing disasters to concern ourselves with!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 1) {
"Good," the king of the underground says. "I will see you all later, then. You can find your way back to the waiting room?"

"We'll manage," Daren says. "Come on, let's go."

Daren leads the way with a stiff limp, and the rest of us follow, leaving Albert behind in low conversation with the king. Back in the waiting room, we settle onto the wooden benches by the teleporter corridor to wait for the healer. In the meantime, Peter hurries off to get our quarters ready, while Breynan turns down a side tunnel without a word, his shoulders rigid with fury.

"Kelrim must have headed back to the quarantine area," Daren says, after scanning the empty hall.

"Oh yeah..." Hadrik rubs the back of his neck, staring at the floor. "I almost forgot about the goblin. Facing him is going to be rough, after what we did to his friends..."

"At least you didn't strike the fatal blow yourself," Daren says quietly, his voice hollow. "When I realized that the goblin I killed was one of Kelrim's friends, I felt like the lowest scum on earth. I never thought I'd feel that way about killing a monster..."

Kate says nothing. Her arms are crossed tight over her chest, eyes fixed on the blank stone of the far wall. Her face is guarded, but the white-knuckled grip on her cloak shows the slaughter still sits in her throat.

}
