ID: B3-Ch03a-Roam
TEXT:

"Are we free to roam around as we please, while we're here?"

"Yes, you may go wherever you wish, aside from a few restricted areas," the king of the underground says. "Those are easily identified by the sentries posted at the doors. You are also free to visit the surface if you like, though I strongly advise against it until you've fully recovered from your recent battle."

"One question," Daren says, shifting his weight with a sharp intake of breath. "Do you have an experienced white mage available to cast a restoration spell on Hadrik and me? I'm too drained from the surface fighting to channel anything stable, and these cracked ribs aren't getting any easier to ignore."

"Certainly," the king says. "I will have one of our healers attend to you as soon as his current rounds permit. In the meantime, you may wait in the anteroom where we met, unless you have any other questions for me."

"That covers it for now," Hadrik says.

"Very well," the king says. "Rest and recover. We will resume our discussions tomorrow, once you have reached your decision."

He pauses, glancing briefly toward Kate before addressing one of the rebels instead.

"One additional matter. Breynan, I know this will seem unfair to you, but you will remain behind tomorrow when our men visit the Overseer."

Breynan stiffens, his eyes widening. "My king?"

"I realize I am overruling your wishes," the king of the underground says in a firmer tone, "but I expect your obedience."

"Sire," Breynan protests, stepping forward from the wall, "of all the people in this stronghold, I know the Overseer best. I have interacted directly with that machine on numerous occasions—"

"Which is precisely why you will not be on that team tomorrow," the king says. "You are too emotionally involved in this matter to keep a cool head. I want negotiations handled with strict discipline, by people who are in no danger of letting feelings cloud their judgment. I have read all of your reports concerning the Overseer, and the delegation will take all of your notes into account. You will not join the contact team, however, and that is final. Is that clear?"

"Yes... your highness," Breynan says, his jaw clamped shut.


#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins != 1) {
"Good," the king of the underground says. "I will see you all later, then. You can find your way back to the waiting room?"

"We'll manage," Daren says. "Come on, let's go."

Daren leads the way with a stiff limp, and the rest of us follow, leaving Albert behind in low conversation with the king. Back in the waiting room, we settle onto the wooden benches by the teleporter corridor to wait for the healer. In the meantime, Peter hurries off to get our quarters ready, while Breynan turns down a side tunnel without a word, his shoulders rigid with fury.

"Kelrim must have headed back to the quarantine area," Daren says, after scanning the empty hall.

"Either that, or he went to look for his friends," Hadrik says.

"Likely the latter, if I think about it," Daren says.

"Forget about the damn goblin!" Arraka snaps from the amulet. "We have far more pressing disasters to concern ourselves with!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_goblins == 1) {
"Good," the king of the underground says. "I will see you all later, then. You can find your way back to the waiting room?"

"We'll manage," Daren says. "Come on, let's go."

Daren leads the way with a stiff limp, and the rest of us follow, leaving Albert behind in low conversation with the king. Back in the waiting room, we settle onto the wooden benches by the teleporter corridor to wait for the healer. In the meantime, Peter hurries off to get our quarters ready, while Breynan turns down a side tunnel without a word, his shoulders rigid with fury.

"Kelrim must have headed back to the quarantine area," Daren says, after scanning the empty hall.

"Oh yeah..." Hadrik rubs the back of his neck, staring at the floor. "I almost forgot about the goblin. Facing him is going to be rough, after what we did to his friends..."

"At least you didn't strike the fatal blow yourself," Daren says quietly, his voice hollow. "When I realized that the goblin I killed was one of Kelrim's friends, I felt like the lowest scum on earth. I never thought I'd feel that way about killing a monster..."

Kate says nothing. Her arms are crossed tight over her chest, eyes fixed on the blank stone of the far wall. Her face is guarded, but the white-knuckled grip on her cloak shows the slaughter still sits in her throat.

}
