ID: B3-Ch03a-Goblins
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch3_monsters == 0) {
"Listen, there was no way we could have known those were good goblins! It wasn't our fault!"

"It was our fault," Daren says, his voice thick with misery. "We should have looked closer. We had every sign right in front of us, and we cut them down anyway. Simply because they were goblins. I am ashamed of myself."

"Forget about the damn goblins!" Arraka snaps from the amulet. "We have far more pressing disasters to concern ourselves with!"

"Like what?" Illuna asks.

"Like the fact that none of you idiots could counteract a simple aura manipulation technique!" Arraka says. "How do you expect to bring down a god if you fold under elementary aura tricks?"

"Bring down a... god?..." Leila's father asks, blinking in bewilderment.

"Explain later," I tell him.

"Listen here, you old hag," Hadrik tells Arraka, "I don't know how spirits fight on the magical plane, but here on the earthen plane, people don't use raw aura as an offensive weapon. It took me decades to develop my aura hardening, and I haven't seen three people in all my travels who could do anything similar."

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_monsters == 1) {
"All we can really do now is try and make it up to him in some way."

"How?" Daren asks, shaking his head. "How do we make amends after cutting his companions down in cold blood? We cannot bring them back from the dead."

"We'll find a way," I say. "Even if we can't undo what happened, there has to be something we can do for him. If we sit down and talk to him—"

"Forget about the damn goblins!" Arraka snaps from the amulet. "We have far more pressing disasters to concern ourselves with!"

"Like what?" Illuna asks.

"Like the fact that none of you idiots could counteract a simple aura manipulation technique!" Arraka says. "How do you expect to bring down a god if you fold under elementary aura tricks?"

"Bring down a... god?..." Leila's father asks, blinking in bewilderment.

"Explain later," I tell him.

"Listen here, you old hag," Hadrik tells Arraka, "I don't know how spirits fight on the magical plane, but here on the earthen plane, people don't use raw aura as an offensive weapon. It took me decades to develop my aura hardening, and I haven't seen three people in all my travels who could do anything similar."

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_monsters == 2) {
"Does it really matter that much? They were all monsters, after all."

"It matters," Daren says sharply, eyes blazing with anger. "If a goblin like Kelrim is willing to risk his life to save total strangers of another race, what about his friends? What if they were just like him? Now we will never know, because we slaughtered them without a second thought. Simply because they were monsters. I am ashamed of myself."

"Forget about the damn goblins!" Arraka snaps from the amulet. "We have far more pressing disasters to concern ourselves with!"

"Like what?" Illuna asks.

"Like the fact that none of you idiots could counteract a simple aura manipulation technique!" Arraka says. "How do you expect to bring down a god if you fold under elementary aura tricks?"

"Bring down a... god?..." Leila's father asks, blinking in bewilderment.

"Explain later," I tell him.

"Listen here, you old hag," Hadrik tells Arraka, "I don't know how spirits fight on the magical plane, but here on the earthen plane, people don't use raw aura as an offensive weapon. It took me decades to develop my aura hardening, and I haven't seen three people in all my travels who could do anything similar."

}
"Hadrik is right," I say. "I've travelled the Western Continent searching for a way to become a mage since I was seventeen. Even the most obscure theoretical grimoires I found had nothing on offensive aura techniques."

"Oh really?" Arraka sneers. "Too bad. Even if dirt-farming mortals can't do it, the gods certainly can. So can Eiden, the golden fox, and Tyrath—though the lizard probably needs his molten lava form to manage it properly. I'd wager Azarius can do it too, along with every sage alive except that incompetent brute Talmak. You can't ignore this. Falling behind in aura control hands your opponent total supremacy. You felt what that frail old royal guard did to you on the bridge. You can't keep relying on me to shout out weaknesses every time an enemy twists the air around you. You need to learn to counter them yourselves."

"And how are we supposed to do that?" I ask.

"Training, obviously," Arraka says. "I will drill it into your thick skulls if it kills me. Mark my words."

"Right," I say. "Speaking of training, I just remembered I have something important to discuss with Leila's father about my stat device."

"Yes, Barry, what is it?" Leila's father asks.

#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2) {
"First off, I was wondering if you could help me unlock my device," I say. "We accidentally triggered the lockout yesterday while trying to access the hidden aura stats."

"Ah!" Leila's father says, smiling faintly. "The aura stats lock. That brings back memories. I triggered that exact safeguard years ago when I first began tinkering with Leila's device to translate her thoughts into words. To answer your question, yes, I can unlock it easily."

"That's great," I say. "Will the hidden stats unlock automatically once you clear it?"

"Not automatically," he says. "But accessing them manually afterward will be straightforward once the device is fixed. I would have made those stats available on Leila's device as well, but I had to permanently deactivate them on her booster in order to allocate processing channels for her magical writing feature."

"Can we do it now?" I ask.

"Right now?" Leila's father asks. "I suppose it wouldn't be a problem. I should probably run some maintenance on Leila's device as well—it has been far too long since I examined the internals. Let us go to my workshop and see what we can do."

"Sounds good," I say, turning to the others. "I'll see you all around in a bit."

}
