choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Mundane, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Mundane)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Mundane
TEXT:

After I wave goodbye to my friends, Leila, her father, and I head down one of the corridors leading out of the waiting room. As we enter the tunnel, Hadrik's voice booms across the stone hall behind us, launching into another tale from his travels.

"Barry, I've always meant to ask you something," Leila writes, glowing blue glyphs hanging in the air between us as we follow her father down the torchlit passage. "How come you never tell us any of your stories, like Hadrik and Daren do all the time?"

"My stories?" I look over at her.

"From your travels," Leila writes. "You mentioned earlier that you've been wandering the Western Continent since you were seventeen."

"Oh," I say. "Well, yes, I've travelled through plenty of towns, but there isn't much to talk about. I spent most of my time in libraries, digging through mouldering manuscripts and old records. Nothing I did compares to Daren or Hadrik's adventures. I'd only bore everyone with mundane research."

"Mundane research?" Leila turns her head, raising an eyebrow. "Didn't you once tell me you learned to fly planes because you spent months test-piloting gliders for a mad gnome inventor in exchange for leads on becoming a mage? And didn't one prototype lose its propeller mid-flight, forcing you to glide down into a pine canopy? That counts as mundane to you?"

"I suppose so," I say. "It's not like I spent my days fighting wyverns or smashing stone golems."

"Just out of curiosity..." Leila writes. "How many more of these 'mundane events' happened to you in the past?"

"Well, let's see," I say. "I worked odd jobs across dozens of cities to pay for food and passage. There was an excavation with an archaeology professor where we nearly got buried alive when a pressure plate tripped in the ruins. I apprenticed under several mages who wanted my theoretical knowledge to help construct new spell matrices, and I lost count of how many experimental charms detonated in our faces. Then there was an expedition with a relic hunter into an old cavern where a goblin warren chased us for miles. We barely made it back to daylight in one piece."

Leila giggles soundlessly.

"I can't believe you claimed to be an 'everyday ordinary guy' before coming to Varathia," she writes. "How is any of what you just described close to ordinary?"

"It's not?" I ask, surprised. "Why wouldn't it be? Sure, it sounds dangerous when you list it all together, but anyone would take a few risks if it meant getting one step closer to their dream."

"No, Barry. That is just you," Leila writes with a smile. "Thank you for telling me, though. I think I'm starting to understand you a little better now."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Travelling, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Travelling)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Travelling
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1) {
"You two seem to be getting along quite well," Leila's father says, glancing back as he works the first lock on his workshop door. "How long have you been travelling together, again?"

"About five days on the road," I say. "Though we spent plenty of time together before the arena, planning tactics and sabotaging the tournament's supplies."

"Right, right..." Leila's father says, searching his pockets for a misplaced key. "So, have you made any plans for the wedding yet?"

"Father!" Leila writes, her eyebrows furrowing sharply in glowing blue light. "It's not like that! Barry is just a friend. And besides, he's already in a relationship with Melindra."

}
#if(v_ch11_romance_rose == 1 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 1 && v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1) {
"You two seem to be getting along quite well," Leila's father says, glancing back as he works the first lock on his workshop door. "How long have you been travelling together, again?"

"About five days on the road," I say. "Though we spent plenty of time together before the arena, planning tactics and sabotaging the tournament's supplies."

"Right, right..." Leila's father says, searching his pockets for a misplaced key. "So, have you made any plans for the wedding yet?"

"Father!" Leila writes, her eyebrows furrowing sharply in glowing blue light. "It's not like that! Barry already has someone waiting for him in Thilias. Her name is Rose, and she's very beautiful."

"Oh, I see!" Leila's father says, finally locating the key. "My mistake, then!"

}
