"Sure you're not," Leila writes, rolling her eyes.

"I'm serious," I say.

"Whatever you say, Barry," Leila writes, unconvinced.

"Shall we go in?" Leila's father asks, pushing the door open.

"After you," Leila writes, and we step inside after him.

Doctor Reidzek closes the heavy oak door behind us. The workshop resembles the underground laboratory King Golmyck maintained beneath his throne room: brass gears, coils of copper wire, and unfinished contraptions cluttering the shelves and corners. In the center sits a broad workbench of oiled timber, bearing a rectangular metal chassis and a neat rack of fine enchanting styluses.

Leila's father moves the rectangular casing aside to clear space on the bench, then gestures toward my pocket.

