#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2 || v_premonition < 4) {
I pull the device from my pocket and hand it over. He sets it on the workbench, draws two sharp metal enchanting tools from a rack beneath the table, and uses them to carefully adjust the aura around the housing.

"Hmm..." Leila's father says. "It has been a while since I worked on an unmodified baseline model. It will take some time to reacquaint myself with the circuitry. Barry, would you mind if I worked on Leila's stat booster first? Her adjustments are minor, but yours will take several hours to complete."

"Go ahead with Leila's device first," I say. "I'm in no rush."

}
#if(v_premonition == 4 && v_b3_ch1_unlock != 2) {
Just as I reach into my pocket, a sudden vision hits me. The room blurs into a haze of disjointed sound. First comes the heavy, metallic rattle of iron chains. Then a young boy's shriek echoes from somewhere in the dark. And right before the vision collapses, Melindra's voice cuts through, sharp and panicked: "Barry, wait! Don't do it!"

The vision snaps shut, leaving me standing in the cold stone room with my pulse racing. None of my previous premonitions have been this vague. What was it trying to warn me about? And when?

"Is everything alright, Barry?" Leila's father asks, watching me stand frozen.

"What?" I shake my head. "Oh—yes. Fine. Here's the device."

I hand him my stat booster and he places it on the workbench. He pulls two sharp metal enchanting tools from a drawer beneath the table and begins carefully manipulating the aura around the casing.

"Hmm..." Leila's father says. "It has been a while since I worked on an unmodified baseline model. It will take some time to reacquaint myself with the circuitry. Barry, would you mind if I worked on Leila's stat booster first? Her adjustments are minor, but yours will take several hours to complete."

"Go ahead with Leila's device first," I say. "I'm in no rush."

}
"Splendid!" Leila's father says, setting my device on a velvet pad. "Leila, hand me your booster, please."

Leila unstraps her stat booster and passes it across. Her father sets it under the oil lamp and begins working with the enchanting styluses, guiding their fine silver tips over the brass surface. For twenty seconds or so, Leila and I watch Doctor Reidzek in complete silence as he makes minute adjustments to the aura channels.

"No need to stand in silence," Leila's father says, glancing up briefly. "A quiet conversation won't break my concentration."

"Oh," Leila writes. "In that case, I'd like to hear more about Barry's life before the tournament. Tell us more about your adventures, Barry!"

"I told you, there isn't much to tell," I say.

"You only say that because you read too many grand histories," Leila writes. "For most people, leaving your home village to explore the continent is an adventure on its own. Tell us about the mages you apprenticed under. There must be at least one interesting story from that time."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Brinhorn, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Brinhorn, v_b3_ch3_stats_unavailable = 1)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Brinhorn
TEXT:

"Now that you mention it, one episode stands out from the rest," I say. "It happened while I was apprenticing for Brinhorn—an old generalist mage who spent decades obsessing over taking the sage title from Azarius. He was remarkably skilled, but no matter how many times he reached the finals of the annual sage trials, he could never close the gap against Azarius. Ironically, from what I heard, the two were old friends, though I never saw them meet during my time under him. Anyway, one morning the old man came to me claiming he had found a solution to both our problems: an ancient artifact powerful enough to turn me into a mage and grant him the edge to unseat Azarius. He was dead wrong."

"The artifact was useless?" Leila writes.

"Worse—it didn't exist," I say. "It was an ambush staged by rivals from the sage trials who were tired of getting eliminated in the semifinals. They wanted Brinhorn out of the picture before the tournament even opened so they could challenge Azarius themselves. We found ourselves trapped in a subterranean labyrinth laced with lethal spell-wards for hours, with virtually no hope of brute-forcing our way out. I had to pull from everything I'd catalogued on inter-elemental interactions just to keep us alive. Fortunately, Brinhorn was a generalist who knew basic incantations across nearly every discipline. All I had to do was direct his casting."

"Direct his casting?" Leila writes, her eyes wide, while her father pauses his styluses to stare across the bench. "You? But... you can't cast magic!"

"Of course I can't," I say. "That didn't stop me from studying thousands of grimoires on spell structures and elemental resonance. Half the manuscripts I read were never published—written by reclusive hedge mages everyone dismissed as crackpots. They were so thrilled someone actually took their theoretical treatises seriously that they let me read their life's work for free just to debate the mechanics over tea. My notebooks are packed with those notes. None of it helped me channel mana on my own, but the theory was sound."

"So..." Leila writes. "How did you escape the labyrinth?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Brinhorn2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Brinhorn2)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Brinhorn2
TEXT:

"Right," I say. "The problem with Brinhorn was that he had a massive repertoire of spells, but only the most basic grasp of their applications. That's why Azarius always dismantled him. Knowing a spell exists isn't enough. The entire advantage of being a generalist is structural flexibility. You can't cast like a specialist and expect to win. A dedicated specialist will always out-power you in their chosen field, but if you weave disciplines together, the combinations are limitless. As long as you understand how each element counterbalances another, you can disassemble almost any static ward. Brinhorn had dozens of spells prepared, but I wasn't confident in his fine control under pressure, so I gave him the simplest framework I could think of: multi-step dispelling."

"Multi... what?" Leila writes.

"Multi-step dispelling," I repeat. "A method I pieced together from older grimoires and those hermit theorists. As long as you can isolate the component spells used to weave a complex ward, you can cast the exact inverted matrices in reverse order to unravel the structure. It wouldn't work against a master duelist like Azarius—he would alter his flow mid-cast to throw you off—but against static traps, you have all the time in the world to analyze the sequence. Even better, reversing the matrix carries minimal rebound risk; if the timing slips, the trap doesn't detonate, so you can adjust your cadence and try again. It took us most of the day for Brinhorn to master the rhythm, but his rivals hadn't waited around to check the bodies, so we walked out intact."

"Wait," Leila writes, staring in disbelief. "You're telling me you're the one who invented the 'Brinhorn dispelling technique'? The breakthrough that revolutionized ward-breaking worldwide and ended up in every standard academy manual?"

"Brinhorn took the credit, but I didn't care," I say. "The concept itself is straightforward: run the incantations, somatic gestures, and aura flow in precise reverse. The difficulty is purely in execution—getting the timing and cadence exact under pressure. And even with the framework clear, Brinhorn and I spent hours deconstructing each ward's layered enchantments before he could begin casting."

"Even so..." Leila writes. "Did Brinhorn never consider giving you credit for your own breakthrough?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Brinhorn3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Brinhorn3)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Brinhorn3
TEXT:

"He asked if he could put his name on it, and I told him to go ahead," I say. "What would he have called it anyway? 'Barry's dispelling technique'? There are thousands of Barrys out there. Who would know which one invented it? What actually annoyed me wasn't the name—it was that Brinhorn spent the next month trying to drag me to academic symposiums to do theoretical research. By then, I already knew scholarship alone would never grant me mana, and that my only real shot was an ancient relic or an artifact. I turned him down until I caught wind of a new rumor, packed my bag, and slipped out of town. I haven't seen him since, though I wouldn't be shocked to run into him here in Varathia. A tournament like this is right up his alley."

"Barry, what you've just told us is extraordinary," Leila's father says. "I had no idea such depth of magical comprehension could be achieved purely from texts."

"It's still incomplete," I say. "I've spent days trying to map the loopholes Eiden uses in his casting, and I'm barely scratching the surface. I can reverse-engineer tricks he's already demonstrated in front of us, but anticipating new ones requires an intuitive feel for mana that textbooks can't provide. Which brings me to what I wanted to ask, Doctor Reidzek. Is there any way to unlock the Magical Knowledge and Magical Power stats along with the aura metrics?"

"If you mean the primary magical parameters, no," Leila's father says, shaking his head. "The aura stats were merely concealed, but the magical stats are locked behind encrypted security keys. Attempting to force them open without the correct passcode risks permanently frying the internal enchantments. I cannot advise it."

choice(""Isn't there a way to crack the code without using brute force?"", B3-Ch03a-Workbench, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Workbench, v_b3_ch3_magical = 0)
choice(""Is there anyone aside from the higher ranked lessathi who might know the code?"", B3-Ch03a-Workbench, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Workbench, v_b3_ch3_magical = 1)
choice(""Why do you think the aura stats are less protected than the magical stats?"", B3-Ch03a-Workbench, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Workbench, v_b3_ch3_magical = 2)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Workbench
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch3_magical == 0) {
"Isn't there a way to crack the code without using brute force?"

"If you restrict yourself to analytical decryption without forcing the nodes, there is no risk of damaging the hardware," Leila's father says. "However, calculating the key through trial and error could take months or years. It depends entirely on luck."

"Let's hope we get lucky, then," I say.

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_magical == 1) {
"Is there anyone aside from the higher ranked lessathi who might know the code?"

"It is possible," Leila's father says. "An assistant working directly under senior researchers might have overheard the passphrase or seen it logged in maintenance registers. That is purely speculation, however. I know of no specific individuals."

"Figured as much," I say. "Thanks anyway."

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_magical == 2) {
"Why do you think the aura stats are less protected than the magical stats?"

"These devices date back centuries, so I cannot speak with certainty," Leila's father says. "My assessment is that they were restricted for very different reasons. The magical stats were originally engineered with the hope of enabling casting for standard lessathi. Half-lessathi were never considered equal to pure-bloods, so strict encryption was implemented to prevent a half-blood from accessing them by chance. The aura metrics, by contrast, were hidden simply because the lessathi abandoned development before safety trials were finalized. That said, based on my diagnostics, the three aura stats appear fully functional, so you need not fear catastrophic failure in the field."

"Right," I say, not entirely reassured.

}
"May I ask," Leila's father says, "why you seek the magical stats? As far as records show, only half-lessathi can interface with those parameters."

"Because I am half-lessathi," I say. "I already triggered them once when the device entered emergency mode, so I know the channels respond."

"You are half-lessathi?" Leila's father blinks in astonishment. "Leila, did you know this?"

"I found out when he did," Leila writes. "He had no idea he carried lessathi blood until he arrived at the tournament."

"Fascinating," Leila's father says, shaking his head slowly. "I have a dozen questions, but I will not consume your afternoon with my curiosity. Leila's booster is calibrated, but yours will require quiet concentration. Could you return in a few hours? I should have the pathways restored by then."

"Of course," Leila writes, taking her stat booster from the bench. "We'll leave you to it. Come on, Barry, back to the waiting room."

We say goodbye to Doctor Reidzek and make our way back down the stone passage. On the way, we pass Peter and a few unfamiliar rebels navigating the corridors, while Leila asks a few more questions about my travels. When we step back into the waiting room, we find Daren and Hadrik fully healed, waiting by the benches.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Technique, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Technique)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Technique
TEXT:

"Oh good, you're back!" Daren says as we approach. "We were just discussing what to do next. What do you think—"

"Did you know Barry is the one who invented the Brinhorn dispelling technique?" Leila writes in glowing blue letters across the air, cutting Daren off mid-sentence.

"What?" Daren blinks, looking toward me with a puzzled frown. "Don't be ridiculous. That technique was developed by Brinhorn. The name is right on it. Barry was joking and you took him seriously."

"I wasn't joking," I say. "I developed the method while apprenticing under him. I told him he could put his name on it because credit didn't matter to me. It's just a technique like any other. If I hadn't mapped it out, someone else would have eventually."

"Barry, what are you—" Daren starts, but a strangled hiss from the golden amulet cuts him short.

"He's not lying..." Arraka rasps, her voice thin and hollow. "I've been monitoring his aura flux the entire time, and there isn't a single wobble. No. No, this is impossible. Not him! Anyone but him!"

"Why are you making such a scene?" Illuna asks. "As he said, it's a logical framework. Anyone with sufficient theoretical grounding could design an inversion matrix without casting ability."

"It is not just a framework!" Arraka shrieks. "I have existed for over ten thousand years and never once conceived of inverted somatic matrices! It was sitting right there, plain as day, and it never crossed my mind! And not for lack of trying! Do you realize how tedious multi-layer ward-breaking was before that technique? I adopted it myself because the sheer elegance was undeniable! And to learn it came from Barry? All this time, I've been casting a spell designed by BARRY? I am a disembodied spirit in an amulet and I feel like vomiting. I can't—ugh—"

Convulsive gagging noises rattle from the golden disc. The others stare in bewilderment, while Daren's face breaks into a broad, disbelieving grin.

"Well, I'll be damned!" Daren laughs, shaking his head. "The Brinhorn technique is the exact framework I used when forging the dispelling enchantments on my sword. And I got it from you... Come here, you sly bastard!"

He hooks my neck in a rough headlock with his heavy forearm and rubs his knuckles vigorously across the top of my scalp.

"What else have you been hiding from us?" Daren grins, scrubbing my hair. "Are you going to tell us next that you coached Azarius through his apprenticeship? Out with it!"

"Knock it off!" I shove his armored arm away, ducking clear. "It wasn't worth bringing up. It happened years ago, and I had no interest in academic research. We needed a way out of a trap-laced maze, so I solved the puzzle. There's no reason to make such a big deal out of it."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Fluke, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Fluke)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Fluke
TEXT:

"Don't listen to this idiot," Arraka says, her voice recovering its usual razor edge. "Whether by accident or design, revolutionizing an entire discipline of defensive magic is not something to scoff at. And I doubt it was an accident. I always wondered how Barry-boy dismantled Eiden's tricks with so little exposure, but everything makes sense now. I don't care if this is latent talent from his half-lessathi blood or the result of single-minded obsession. We are putting it to work. Change of plans, everyone! Barry is officially our trump card. Unlocking his magical stats comes before everything else. I won't take 'no' for an answer. We put our heads together every day until we find a way to breach the code. Understood?"

For the next half hour, at Arraka's insistence, we debate methods to get my magical stats working. I explain that Leila's father cannot force the channels, so we throw out increasingly outlandish schemes to acquire the passcode. The debate quickly runs in circles, burning energy without producing any meaningful progress.

"Listen, Arraka," Daren says, sighing as he leans back against the stone wall. "We've had this exact exchange three times. We have no better choice. Why can't we stick with our original plan of methodical decryption using my enchanting tools?"

"Because manual decryption takes too long!" Arraka snaps. "The next trial could arrive tomorrow for all we know. We cannot waste weeks fumbling in the dark. That is why I've been trying to drag a functional alternative out of you people for the past half hour!"

"We've covered every angle," Daren says. "There is no shortcut. Can we focus on something we can actually accomplish, instead of chasing the impossible?"

"Think like that, and don't act surprised when that magic-less loser shoots past you and leaves you in the dust," Arraka sneers. "If that moron proves anything, it's that even the impossible yields if your determination borders on insanity."

"I never thought I'd see the day when Arraka gave Barry an honest compliment," Hadrik says, grinning broadly. "Barry, looks like you're moving up in the world!"

choice(""Well, it's the least she could do after using one of my techniques without my permission."", B3-Ch03a-Permission, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Permission)
choice(""She did? That's funny. I could have sworn that she only just called me a loser and a moron."", B3-Ch03a-Moron, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Moron)
choice(""There truly is no greater purpose in life than to be praised by a spirit in an amulet."", B3-Ch03a-Purpose, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Purpose)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Permission
TEXT:

"Well, it's the least she could do after using one of my techniques without my permission."

"Very funny, Barry-boy," Arraka says. "Laugh now. Once those stats unlock, I will drill you through casting matrices day and night."

"You think you'll need to force me?" I say. "The day those stats unlock, you'll see me practicing spells in my sleep."

"Listen," Daren says, cutting in. "If we're done racking our brains, I'd rather return to our plans regarding the king of the underground and the rebels. What do you think about the delegation to the Overseer? Should we go along with it or not?"

"I recommend waiting before making a decision," Kate says, her arms crossed. "We don't have enough information yet to reach a proper conclusion."

"A delay won't hurt," Daren agrees. "The king left us time to decide until tomorrow."

He pauses, meeting our eyes in turn.

"We will think it over tonight and make our decision by morning at the latest. But whatever we decide, it needs to be unanimous. Everyone agree?"

"Aye," we all say in unison.

"Good," Daren says. "Now that that's settled, I'm heading back to the quarantine ward. Arraka, you offered to help with the plague. Does that still stand?"

"Yeah, yeah," Arraka says. "Take me there, and we'll see what's what."

"Here she is," Illuna says, unhooking the amulet and passing it to Daren. "Keep a constant eye on her. If you don't pay attention, she'll try to free herself in no time."

"I'll be careful," Daren says. "Though building an explosion from inside the casing takes hours, doesn't it?"

"Under normal conditions, yes, but she has older tricks we don't know about," Illuna says. "Keep an open mind."

"Right," Daren says.

"Tsk, tsk, tsk," Arraka mocks as Daren holds the golden chain, heading down the corridor. "Such little trust, Illuna. I thought I raised you better than this!"

"See you all later," Daren calls over his shoulder, while Illuna ignores Arraka completely. "Call me on the transceiver if anything important comes up!"

"Aye, we will!" Hadrik calls back. "Good luck!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Recording, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Recording)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Moron
TEXT:

"She did? That's funny. I could have sworn that she only just called me a loser and a moron."

"Nah, she was only joking," Hadrik says with a chuckle. "A real comedian, that Arraka!"

"My mistake," I say. "Her humor must have been too subtle for me."

"Listen," Daren says, cutting in. "If we're done racking our brains, I'd rather return to our plans regarding the king of the underground and the rebels. What do you think about the delegation to the Overseer? Should we go along with it or not?"

