choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Recording, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Recording)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Recording
TEXT:

"I suppose we should decide on our next move as well," I say. "Should we take a look around, or—"

"There you are!" Peter says, hurrying toward us down the tunnel. "I wanted to let you know your quarters are ready. Unfortunately, we couldn't secure separate rooms for each of you, so we prepared a larger communal room instead. I hope that won't be an issue."

"Not at all," I say, looking toward the others. "We've been sleeping in tents across most of Varathia. We weren't expecting much luxury."

"That is a relief!" Peter says. "Would you like to take your backpacks to the room now?"

"We'll hold onto them for now, thanks," I say, as the rest of our group adjusts their straps.

"Oh, alright," Peter says. "In that case, is there anything else I can help you with?"

"Yes," Kate says. "I'm curious about something. Your king of the underground mentioned listening devices within the palace, but how do you retrieve the recordings? Isn't it dangerous to retrieve them manually every time?"

"We don't need to retrieve them manually," Peter says. "We maintain a direct link to the devices from a room down here, listening to the palace in real time. That is also where we make the recordings."

"Didn't you tell us earlier that the seredium strata block all magical resonance from reaching the surface?" Kate asks.

"In most areas, yes, but the recording chamber is different," Peter says. "Come, let me take you there and you can see for yourselves."

"Are you sure we're permitted in there?" Leila writes. "We wouldn't want to get in trouble with the king and his guards."

"I may not look imposing, but I still carry some authority here," Peter says. "I am one of the three founders of the original resistance, alongside Albert and Breynan. Even if we follow the king's orders nowadays, I like to believe we retain a little independence. You don't need to worry about trouble. If anything happens, I'll take full responsibility. Besides, our microphones are placed in desolate, abandoned halls, so there's little chance of overhearing anything sensitive anyway."

"Lead the way, then," I say.

"The recording room is close by," Peter says. "Follow me."

As we follow Peter out, Hadrik remains seated in the waiting room.

"Hadrik, are you coming?" I ask.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Corner, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Corner)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Corner
TEXT:

"Go on ahead without me," Hadrik says. "I'm still a little worn out from the fight on the bridge, and I'd rather rest my legs."

"Understood," I say, and we follow Peter down one of the corridors to the left.

Less than half a minute later, Peter stops before a heavy iron-banded door and pulls a ring of brass keys from his pocket.

"Already?" I ask.

"I told you it was close," Peter says, turning a key in the lock. "Let's go."

He pulls the door open, revealing a stone staircase spiraling upward. After climbing through a narrow shaft, we reach an iron trapdoor. Peter unlatches it, and we climb into a chamber packed with large metal consoles, glowing dials, and flashing crystal indicators. A technician was sitting at the central desk playing with a small virtual pet device, which he quickly slips into his coat when we emerge.

"Peter!" the man says nervously. "I wasn't expecting you up here. Is there something you need, or—"

"Relax, Bill," Peter says, lowering the trapdoor once we're all inside. "No inspection today. I'm just showing our guests where we handle the monitoring. As you can see, this room sits at a higher elevation than the rest of the base, beneath a much thinner vein of seredium. Unfortunately, our listening devices are confined to deserted corners of the castle to prevent discovery. As a result, we rarely pick up active conversations, but whenever a signal comes through, the receivers record it automatically—"

"Can you confirm this is the area where you would like to have the conversation?" a flat metallic drone suddenly crackles from a speaker on the central console.

"Yes," Eiden's calm voice floats through the grill. "Over here, in this empty corner would be perfect."

"Request acknowledged," the drone states. "Please stand by. The Overseer protocol will be initiated shortly."

choice(""I don't get it. Is this happening right now, or is it a recording?"", B3-Ch03a-Happening, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Happening)
choice(""Looks like the Overseer isn't the only one who knows where the listening devices are."", B3-Ch03a-Listening, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Listening)
choice(""Eiden sure isn't hiding the fact that he's constantly watching us, is he?"", B3-Ch03a-Constantly, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Constantly)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Happening
TEXT:

"I don't get it. Is this happening right now, or is it a recording?"

"It's live, but... I don't understand," Peter says, staring at the brass console in disbelief. "The listening device transmitting this feed is planted in an abandoned cellar beneath the castle foundations. We've never intercepted a voice from that room before. Why would they hold a conversation in such a place?"

"Take a wild guess," Illuna murmurs, her blue eyes narrowing.

"Quiet, please!" Bill mutters frantically, adjusting dials on the brass board. "I'm trying to make sure the recording device catches every syllable. The king will have my head if we lose this feed!"

"The Overseer protocol is now being activated," the metallic voice announces through the grill. "We apologize for the delay."

"Oh, it's no problem," Eiden says pleasantly. "Take all the time you need."

After a few moments of static, the synthetic voice speaks again, but this time its tone shifts with subtle inflections, sounding much closer to a human cadence.

"Interesting," the Overseer says. "From analyzing the logs of this unit, it would appear you did not arrive in this place by chance, but went out of your way to guide this unit into the empty corner of a deserted cellar. Is that correct?"

"Yes," Eiden says. "I felt the air down here was remarkably fresh compared to the rest of the castle. Wouldn't you agree?"

"Processing..." the Overseer responds. "After analyzing your previous statement, as well as several comments logged in the past, the Overseer protocol has concluded you prefer to use sarcasm as your primary method of communication. Is that correct?"

Eiden laughs softly.

"I like your direct approach," he says. "Yes, I suppose you could say that is correct, for the most part."

"Thank you for your confirmation," the Overseer says. "The Overseer protocol is now attempting to adapt to your particular conversational style. This may take a while, as the denizens of this castle seem incapable of grasping the concept of sarcasm, so there aren't many recent examples available for analysis."

"Of course," Eiden says. "Take your time."

"Requesting clarification," the Overseer says, after five seconds of silence. "When referring directly to the king of Ollendor, you seem to have often used the syntagm 'oh great and wise king'. Judging by the subtle inflections in your tone, it would appear you have always used it in a sarcastic manner, although the king has never noticed this. Is that correct?"

"And what would you do with that information, if I were to confirm it?" Eiden asks.

"The Overseer protocol assures you this data will be utilized strictly for analysis and will remain completely confidential," the Overseer states. "There is no danger of information leaks."

"Oh?" Eiden muses. "Should I assume, then, that the conversation we are having right now will also remain confidential?"

"The Overseer protocol will never disclose private discourse to outside parties," the Overseer replies. "The protocol's only purpose is to provide assistance. However, it must be stated that the Overseer protocol cannot control the flow of information resulting from anyone listening in on this conversation at this very moment."

"That is not a problem," Eiden says. "And the answer to your initial question is 'yes'."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Opinion, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Opinion)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Listening
TEXT:

"Looks like the Overseer isn't the only one who knows where the listening devices are."

"The timing is too deliberate," Kate says quietly, her breath frosting the air. "He knew exactly where to stand."

"I don't understand," Peter says. "What are you two talking about?"

"Quiet, please!" Bill mutters frantically, adjusting dials on the brass board. "I'm trying to make sure the recording device catches every syllable. The king will have my head if we lose this feed!"

