choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Exploring, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Exploring)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Exploring
TEXT:

"I will go the opposite way, then," Kate says.

"And I'll be going with you," Melindra tells Kate.

"You will?" Kate asks, glancing over with narrow suspicion.

"I did tell you yesterday that I would help you with sharpening the aura of your ice magic, didn't I?" Melindra says.

"You're right, I forgot," Kate says. "In that case, let's try that tunnel over there."

"Right behind you, icey," Melindra says, as the two of them head down the corridor.

"And I'll just... continue to sit here... doing some training..." Hadrik mutters from his stool.

"I think I'll go back to my father's workshop," Leila writes to me. "I've always loved just standing next to him, watching him work, and it's been too long since I've had the opportunity. What about you, Barry? What are you planning to do in the next few hours?"

"Well, I can't really do much without my stat device, so I'll probably just go exploring for a bit," I say.

"Alright," Leila writes. "See you later, Barry!"

"See you," I say, as Leila turns down the passage toward the workshop.

"Later, Hadrik," I say, and head down an unexplored corridor.

For the next few hours, I walk the rebel tunnels, mapping out the layout in my head. The complex is divided into distinct operational sectors. There are the living quarters, where fighters sleep in staggered shifts, and the communal mess hall, where rebels and the king's men share meals in guarded quiet. Further back sits an infirmary and a small recreation area. Beyond those sectors, armed sentries with grounded halberds bar every iron door leading to command chambers and storage vaults.

As I wander, I pass several familiar faces: the little girl whose parents were taken, and the two men who carried the sick elder to the quarantine zone. In a side cavern, I run into Flower and Illuna busy with elemental drills. Before they spot me, I hear them bickering. Flower is still attempting Kate's flat, grave tone to sound like an adult, and Illuna is growing visibly exasperated. When they notice me, they break off to wave, though I have no doubt they resumed their argument the moment my footsteps faded.

The tunnels repeatedly loop back to the central waiting room. On one pass, I run into Daren and Arraka, who are returning from the infirmary carrying several lessathi diagnostic devices. We stop briefly near the archway to trade news.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03a-Dagger, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Dagger)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Dagger
TEXT:

"We've been progressing slowly, but at a steady pace," Daren tells me, adjusting the weight of the brass diagnostic tools under his arm. "I'm still struggling to figure out how to have a healing spell treat a person's aura directly, but these older lessathi devices might give me some hints. What about you? Did you find anything useful while exploring?"

"Not really," I say. "Sentries block every secure area, so I've only seen the standard barracks and mess halls."

"Has Hadrik been sitting like that ever since I entered the quarantine zone?" Daren whispers, nodding toward the dwarf, who is still seated in the same wooden chair, staring gloomily at the stone floor.

"Yeah, he hasn't moved an inch," I say. "I think his mood has something to do with what Gontrok said earlier about the dwarf village from all those years ago. The giant struck an old nerve."

"Do you think it's related to his trial from the God of Time's ritual?" Daren asks quietly.

"It's possible," I say. "Either way, he won't tell us anything if we press him. We have to wait for him to bring it up on his own."

"Yeah, you're probably right," Daren says, sighing. "Well, I'm off. Have fun exploring."

"I'll try," I say. "Good luck with curing the blight."

We split up, and I head down another corridor I haven't fully checked yet. After walking the damp passages and testing heavy doors for fifteen minutes, I pause at an intersection to get my bearings.

The passage is dead quiet—until a man's voice speaks from right behind my shoulder.

"I noticed you've been having quite a bit of fun exploring our rebel base, Barry," Albert says. "See anything you like?"

A cold prickle runs down my spine. The hallway was completely empty a second ago. The soundless approach brings Wilhelm from the Thilias arena instantly to mind.

I turn around. My eyes drop straight to the dagger hanging from his belt, its orange-wrapped hilt confirming my suspicions.

"A fine dagger, isn't it?" Albert says, tracking my gaze with a faint smile. "You couldn't ask for a more perfect weapon as an assassin. In civilian hands, it functions like any kitchen knife, but our guild trains us from childhood to make full use of its properties. The way it focuses aura concealment is extraordinary. Still, you didn't flinch the way most do when I closed the distance. Have you encountered an assassin up close before, Barry?"

"Yes," I say, keeping my voice level and my hands free. "There was an assassin in my division when I was fighting in the arena of Thilias. His name was Wilhelm."

"Wilhelm..." Albert says, a thoughtful glimmer in his eyes. "The name is familiar. I believe I've heard of an operative by that name, though I never had the honor of meeting him. Were the two of you friends?"

"Not exactly," I say. "He tried to kill me."

"You were targeted by an assassin, and yet you survived?" Albert asks, genuinely intrigued. "How did you manage such a feat?"

choice(""It's because Wilhelm was a wimp. Just like every other assassin."", B3-Ch03a-Affiliation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Affiliation, v_b3_ch3_assassin = 0)
choice(""It's because I am simply unbeatable."", B3-Ch03a-Affiliation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Affiliation, v_b3_ch3_assassin = 1)
choice(""There were far bigger threats than an assassin on that battlefield, I assure you."", B3-Ch03a-Affiliation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Affiliation, v_b3_ch3_assassin = 2)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Affiliation
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch3_assassin == 0) {
"It's because Wilhelm was a wimp. Just like every other assassin."

"A bold claim," Albert says with a polite smile. "Yet surely you must have had at least a little trouble dealing with him..."

"Nah, he was weak, the way every assassin is weak," I say. "He didn't have what it takes to fight me face to face, so he relied on petty tricks and paid the price. That's all there is to it."

"I see," Albert says, his smile unchanging. "Let us hope the two of us never need to cross blades in the future, then. I would hate to have to prove you wrong."

"I hope so too," I say. "For your sake."

Albert nods with the same calm smile and walks past me. When I turn around, the corridor is completely empty. He slipped away without a sound. I make a mental note to watch my back around him—he is noticeably sharper and more dangerous than Wilhelm.

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_assassin == 1) {
"It's because I am simply unbeatable."

"And very modest as well, it would seem!" Albert says.

"Of course," I say. "Even when it comes to modesty, nobody can surpass me!"

"No doubt," Albert says. "Well, if you truly are as unbeatable as you say, let us hope we never become enemies. I would hate to have to prove you wrong."

He smiles politely and walks past me down the corridor. When I turn around to look for him, he is already gone. I make a mental note to watch my back around him—he is noticeably sharper and more dangerous than Wilhelm.

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_assassin == 2) {
"There were far bigger threats than an assassin on that battlefield, I assure you."

"Still..." Albert says, "dealing with an assassin requires an entirely different skillset than what you need to defeat beasts or gladiators. Surviving an assassination attempt is definitely an impressive achievement. You have my sincere congratulations."

"You're not going to try to kill me too, are you?" I ask.

Albert laughs softly.

"Only if you force me to, Barry," he says. "However, at this point in time we are allies, not enemies, so you don't need to worry about such things. Anyway, I think I've held you long enough. Have fun with your exploring. I'm sure we will be seeing each other again soon."

Albert smiles politely and walks past me. When I turn around, he is already gone. I make a mental note to watch my back around him—he is noticeably sharper and more dangerous than Wilhelm.

}
After spending twenty more minutes walking the corridors, I run into Peter, who tells me he's been searching for me and that the king of the underground has requested an immediate meeting with our group. When I ask what this is about, he says he isn't allowed to discuss it, but the nervous strain on his face is unmistakable. I call everyone on their transceivers, and we assemble in the waiting room before heading back to the throne chamber.

Inside the hall, we seat ourselves on the same wooden chairs prepared for us earlier. Albert is present as well, leaning against the stone wall with arms crossed, but Peter hovers near the entrance, visibly fidgeting. The only people missing from the morning meeting are Breynan and Leila's father.

"Thank you for coming on such short notice," the king of the underground says from his throne. "I know you are all busy, so I will keep this brief. Tell me... in what way is your team associated with this 'Creator' who goes by the name of Eiden?"

"Why do you ask?" Daren asks.

"Oh, that's right, you were in the quarantine zone this whole time, so you don't know what happened," the king says. "In short, I noticed that this 'Eiden' has been going out of his way lately to provide your group with operational intelligence regarding the king of Ollendor and his clique. I noticed this first when my men reported that Eiden delayed the public blessing ceremony for no apparent reason, only to give the go-ahead shortly after your group arrived in the Perellion plaza. Since that was one of the few places where the screening was scheduled, it made me suspicious. However, the confirmation arrived a few hours ago, when I received word that your friends happened to be in our recording room at the exact moment Eiden was talking to the Overseer, and Eiden more or less admitted he lured the drone there on purpose because he knew our listening device was planted nearby. So I ask you again... What exactly is your affiliation with Eiden?"

choice(""Does this whole situation seem familiar to anyone else, or is it just me?"", B3-Ch03a-Familiar, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Familiar)
choice(""We know him, but we wouldn't really call him a friend."", B3-Ch03a-Friend, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Friend)
choice(""He's helped us before, but he mostly does it on a whim."", B3-Ch03a-Whim, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03a-Whim)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Familiar
TEXT:

"Does this whole situation seem familiar to anyone else, or is it just me?"

"I beg your pardon?" the king of the underground asks, frowning.

"Excuse my companion," Daren says, holding up a hand. "He mentions it because you are not the first monarch to pose that exact question since we set foot on Varathia. There is no simple answer. Eiden has posed as a tournament participant since the opening trials, crossing paths with multiple groups, including ours. At times he has intervened to keep us alive; at others, he has tried to kill us with a smile on his face. Knowing when he acts as an ally or an adversary is impossible. He remains completely unpredictable."

"I find that difficult to accept," the king says, leaning back. "It seems far more plausible that you and the 'Creator' are acting in concert, and you simply wish to conceal that alliance from us."

"We are not conspiring with anyone!" Daren insists. "Our sole purpose in Ollendor is to eliminate the king. We share the same goal."

"Do we?" Kate says sharply. She stares directly at the king, her eyes cold.

"What do you mean?" the king asks.

"An ally doesn't send an assassin to shadow our steps," Kate says, gesturing toward Albert. "Your agent has tailed us across these halls all day. Melindra also tells us you've managed questionable operations over the years—assassinations, slave trafficking, black markets. Who is to say the 'questionable morality' Eiden mentioned to the Overseer wasn't built by your own hands?"

"A fair point," the king says calmly. "Though I remind you that slave trading carries no moral condemnation on this continent, whatever your views beyond Varathia. As for the assassinations, many were carried out on my command, though every death served the stabilization of our society. That said, I can no more prove my integrity to you than you can prove you aren't working for Eiden. Trust on that front is exhausted."

"What do you propose, then?" Kate asks.

"A simpler arrangement," the king smiles faintly. "An alliance founded on necessity rather than trust. Both sides utilize each other to advance their objectives, with no expectation of loyalty if interests diverge. Anything goes. Frankly, your group anticipated this outcome hours ago—why else would you still carry full packs on your backs when quarters were assigned this morning?"

"You're not wrong," Daren says slowly. "What about the Overseer delegation tomorrow? Does the invitation stand?"

"My delegation proceeds as planned tomorrow morning," the king says. "What you do with that information is entirely your decision."

"Understood," Daren says. "Is our audience concluded? Can we return to our business?"

"Certainly," the king says. "You retain full freedom of movement within our tunnels. I will instruct Albert to end his surveillance as a gesture of good faith. And Peter... next time you escort guests into restricted listening arrays, inform me in advance. Will you?"

"Yes... your highness," Peter mutters, looking at the floor.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Complicate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Complicate)


ID: B3-Ch03a-Friend
TEXT:

"We know him, but we wouldn't really call him a friend."

"If he is not your friend, why provide you with intelligence?" the king of the underground asks.

"Hard to say," I tell him. "Eiden operates on whims. Sometimes he steps in to help, and sometimes he tries to slaughter us with a smile on his face. Even when he looks like he's helping us, he's usually setting up a game we haven't figured out yet."

"I find that difficult to accept," the king says, leaning back. "It seems far more plausible that you and the 'Creator' are acting in concert, and you simply wish to conceal that alliance from us."

